```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a crashed FRR daemon (bgpd/frr.service) on leaf_router_0_0, not a
physical cabling issue. All three interfaces on leaf_router_0_0 (eth0, eth1, eth2)
are UP and carrier-active, ruling out the team's hypothesis about a downed fabric
cable.

The frr.service on leaf_router_0_0 is in "failed" state. The FRR log shows that at
03:26:24 UTC the bgpd process hit an internal error and shut down — zebra logged
"Client 'bgp' encountered an error and is shutting down" for both bgpd sessions and
immediately withdrew all BGP routes from the kernel RIB. With bgpd down, no BGP
sessions are established toward spine_router_0_0 (172.16.0.4) or spine_router_0_1
(172.16.0.8), and no routes are learned. The routing table on leaf_router_0_0 shows
only directly connected prefixes; it has no route to 10.0.1.0/24, so the first-hop
router responds to pc_0_0 with ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" for every packet
toward pc_0_1. The rest of the fabric is healthy — both spines have active BGP
sessions and carry a valid BGP route to 10.0.1.0/24 via leaf_router_0_1.

Root cause: the bgpd process on leaf_router_0_0 crashed, taking the FRR service into
a failed state and removing all learned BGP routes.

Corrective fix: run `systemctl restart frr` on leaf_router_0_0. The on-disk
configuration in /etc/frr/frr.conf is correct and intact (AS 65200, both spine
neighbors, network 10.0.0.0/24 advertised), so a plain service restart will restore
BGP sessions and re-populate the routing table within seconds.
=== END ===
